{
  "abstract": "How many changes to the classical no-three-in-line construction can be applied independently on one integer board? Let $p$ tend to infinity through primes congruent to five modulo eight, and let $t=\\lfloor(\\log_2\\log_2p)^{1/4}\\rfloor$. On almost every nonsquare hyperbola level, deleting at most $(\\log p)^{1+o(1)}$ native points prepares $t$ exchanges, each replacing two native points by $t$ central targets. Every subset of the exchanges yields an ordinary cap. All target-pair joins miss the field hyperbola, and native secants through the targets avoid short primitive directions and short endpoint ratios. Counting physical joins between groups gives a larger family. One can retain all but $O(p/\\log\\log p)$ of the classical frame and support $p/(\\log p)^{1+o(1)}$ independent two-for-$t$ exchanges, with both prescribed secants through every target present before activation. The proof counts mixed ordinary triples on moving conics across levels by solving two quadratic equations for each direction and step pair. At prime 1,000,037 a ten-exchange example admits all 1,024 selections, including 198 target-pair lines that meet the native hyperbola in the field but miss it on the integer board. A matching certifies the need for 583 preparatory deletions. The proved size increases are relative to the prepared frame. An improvement of the classical asymptotic density remains open.",
